    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Isaac Paredes** (66.57) and **Brice Turang** (63.14) headline our offensive output as the only two hitters clearing the 60-point threshold. **Isaac Paredes** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Grayson Rodriguez; the 16% SP skill adjustment and a 9% dynamic platoon edge make him our highest-upside play at the hot corner. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** remains a tactical necessity at MI. While facing a tough arm in George Kirby, his placement at the top of the order generates a massive 10% efficiency bump, keeping his floor high despite the quality of the opposing starter.\n\nThe decision to keep **Vinnie Pasquantino** (48.79) on the bench was the most difficult call of the morning. While he projects as a reliable source of contact, the optimizer ultimately pivoted toward the middle-order potential of **Josh Naylor** (55.92) and the positional versatility of **Max Muncy** (55.33). **Vinnie Pasquantino** was penalized heavily by a 15% dynamic platoon disadvantage against the lefty Gage Jump, a variable that rendered his raw base projection insufficient to crack the starting seven. We are also monitoring **Jose Caballero** (57.99) closely; his 42% SB floor remains a vital component of our strategy, but his score is being suppressed by a 13% platoon penalty, putting his efficiency at risk if the game flow forces him into unfavorable counts.\n\nWe are currently operating under a \"Lineup Pending\" status for all starters, which introduces volatility to our projected batting order adjustments. We remain cautious of **Austin Riley** (56.26), whose bottom-third order placement\u2014resulting in a 6% score deduction\u2014leaves him as a high-risk, high-reward play against Anthony Kay. Beyond the starting lineup, we are holding steady with our inactive players; **James Wood** remains on the IL, and with no game scheduled for **Mickey Moniak**, our bench flexibility is restricted. Ensure you check for official lineup cards to confirm the expected #1 through #9 placements, as any movement downward in the order for **Brice Turang** or **Isaac Paredes** would necessitate an immediate re-evaluation of our efficiency rankings.",
